implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST: create packages/moe-daemon/src/server/webSocketOrigin.test.ts.

Harness: follow server/runnerChannel.test.ts:
- a temp project with StateManager and McpAdapter;
- a real `createServer()` listening on 127.0.0.1:0;
- `new MoeWebSocketServer(httpServer, state, adapter)`;
- close everything in afterEach.
Use two helpers:
- `rawUpgrade(path, headerLines)`: net.connect, write exact bytes, resolve the first response line;
- a 'ws' client helper that resolves 'open' or the 'unexpected-response' status.

REFUSED rows. Each expects the status line `HTTP/1.1 403` and a response body that never contains the origin value. For /ws and /mcp, with `vi.spyOn(server as any, 'onConnection')`, assert onConnection is NEVER called: no snapshot is sent and no MCP client is attached.
- Origin 'https://example.com', on /ws and on /mcp;
- Origin 'null';
- an empty Origin value (raw `Origin: \r\n`; present means `!== undefined`, never truthiness);
- a mixed-case header name (`oRiGiN: https://example.com`; Node lowercases names);
- an Origin on /runner WITH a valid runner key: still 403, because the rule applies on every path.

ACCEPTED rows (no Origin), one per real client:
- 'ws' `new WebSocket(url)` with no options (moe-proxy, VS Code, wrapper RPC): opens on /ws and /mcp;
- the claude-plugin's exact raw bytes from packages/moe-claude-plugin/src/daemonClient.ts:291-297 (GET, Host, `Upgrade: websocket`, `Connection: Upgrade`, Sec-WebSocket-Key, `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13`): `HTTP/1.1 101`;
- Node's global WebSocket (`globalThis.WebSocket`; skip when undefined): opens on /mcp. Probed at planning: Node v24's handshake carries no Origin;
- the Java-WebSocket 1.6.0 header set (moe-jetbrains/build.gradle.kts pins org.java-websocket:Java-WebSocket:1.6.0): Host, Upgrade, Connection, Sec-WebSocket-Key, Sec-WebSocket-Version. Confirm the exact names, order and casing against the 1.6.0 source (Draft_6455 and WebSocketClient) and cite it in a test comment. Expect `HTTP/1.1 101`;
- /runner without an Origin still authenticates by its key: refused without the key, accepted with it. Reuse runnerChannel.test.ts's key helpers (ensureRunnerKey, RUNNER_KEY_HEADER).

Run `npx vitest run src/server/webSocketOrigin.test.ts` in the snapshot (step 4) and confirm that only the refusal rows fail before step 2.

Docs, at the problem-class level with no exploit walkthrough. Both files are planned by parked integration rows (66e8d7d1, b621ddc9, ced85748, f3f364a6, f4811a72), so:
- check that `git status --porcelain -- docs/CONFIGURATION.md docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md` is empty first;
- call moe.declare_files for both before the first Edit;
- edit with the Edit tool;
- if a peer's hunks are dirty there, coordinate before editing.

(1) docs/CONFIGURATION.md, '### Daemon Configuration': add a short paragraph:
- the daemon refuses any WebSocket upgrade (/ws, /mcp, /runner) that carries an Origin header, with HTTP 403, before any message is processed;
- no Moe client sends Origin (the IDE plugins, moe-proxy, the Claude plugin hook, the local runner), while browsers always do;
- the allowed set is empty and has no setting or environment knob.

(2) docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md: add a '### WebSocket upgrade refused with HTTP 403' entry where the connection problems live (near 'Plugin can't connect to daemon' or under MCP/Proxy Issues). Give:
- the symptom: a client's handshake gets 403, and the daemon log shows 'Refused WebSocket upgrade: Origin not allowed';
- the cause: the client sent an Origin header, as browser-hosted or custom clients do;
- the fix: connect from a non-browser client without Origin. Browser clients are not supported.

VERIFY, once and fresh: `cd packages/moe-daemon && npm test`, the full daemon suite, because WebSocketServer is shared transport. The checkout's node_modules are Windows builds, so run it in a Linux-native snapshot, per `mem:gotcha-daemon-linux-snapshot-gate` and `mem:gotcha-linux-vitest-reuse-native-node-modules`:
- `git archive HEAD` into a scratch dir;
- copy in this task's 2 source files, cmp-equal to the worktree;
- link or install a native Linux node_modules whose lock matches HEAD;
- run under env -i with a Linux node on PATH and NO_COLOR=1.
Also run `npx tsc --noEmit` there.

Record the file and test counts. Before claiming green, attribute any failure outside the touched files with git log. This command's fresh output is the complete_task verification evidence.
